{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 claims formal verification 'eliminates hidden correlations by fiat,' but formal verification cannot verify the correctness of its own assumptions relative to the real world. The mechanism conflates formal-internal isolation (proved under a spec) with de facto independence of failure modes. A shared spec flaw, shared environmental assumption, or shared compiler/kernel CVE outside the verification boundary can still correlate all isolation domains simultaneously. The target's own disanalogy admits this ('assumptions may be incomplete'), yet the claim still asserts elimination of hidden correlations. This is a causal-mechanism failure: the stated mechanism does not causally produce the claimed independence.","counterexample":["A large service uses formally verified isolation domains with different OS kernels, but all domains share a verified network stack assuming TCP cannot be injected; an attacker injects TCP streams and all domains fail simultaneously despite verified isolation."],"target":"2e9f370185b100400d24d46f3e0fa4ecaee36929472c68518b6f4bd230883cc7"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002's mechanism asserts that decentralized networks 'naturally avoid' a monoculture of assumptions via multiple independent design processes, but it supplies no causal mechanism forcing design diversity. The claim is asserted as default, then the countercondition that decentralized nodes share a common open-source foundation encoding the same assumptions is admitted. This means the proferred causal story (independent design processes) can be defeated by the very mechanism it criticizes (shared assumptions). The target never identifies the distinguishing evidence the problem demands for apparent independence vs shared-cause correlation, beyond listing it as an observation countercondition.","counterexample":["Every decentralized node independently chooses the same widely-used TLS library containing a latent Heartbleed-class bug; design processes are nominally independent but the failure is fully correlated."],"target":"517a86ce6dec97dd0100a1359fd641312fcb880c080452eaa083652a9b42ca2d"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003's causal mechanism for large-service resilience is that governance can 'mandate disjoint dependencies across internal redundancy domains.' But mandating disjoint dependencies does not causally guarantee independence of failure modes: the mandate itself is a single centralized design decision, and a flaw in the governance model, the verification of disjointness, or a shared environmental assumption can still correlate all domains. The target treats governance enforcement as an exogenous guarantee, but under the problem's standard it is another potential shared cause. The countercondition for governance failure is listed, which concedes the mechanism is not sufficient without empirical evidence the governance succeeds.","counterexample":["Governance mandates disjoint OS kernels and compilers, but all domains share a single power grid and a single upstream NTP source; a synchronized time failure corrupts all domains at once."],"target":"8cb9994435bf5a139eb0ea1400cc882f4a1930878e34c14c65299cb18e8cbe6a"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_04 asserts 'strictly more resilient' decentralized design diversity as a natural property, but the mechanism is only 'different teams will naturally exhibit design diversity.' This is a correlation heuristic, not a causal mechanism for independence. The target itself admits a countercondition: all decentralized operators using the same open-source library negates design independence. Since that countercondition is a realistic causal pathway, the 'strictly more resilient' claim is not entailed by the mechanism; it depends on unstated evidence that natural diversity actually persists under real-world convergence pressures.","counterexample":["A decentralized network of independently operated services all independently adopt the same popular cloud SDK because it is cheapest; a latent bug in the SDK causes simultaneous correlated failure, identical to the monoculture SRC_04 attributes only to large services."],"target":"c3ec0a091d831ab26cb4102687061a80388507e7e8f9bbf5f496bc6e405c9d7a"}]}